molecular clouds are the birthplace of  stars and planets but how these  diaphanous fogs of gas collapse into  burning balls of hydrogen is not well  understood part of the problem is that  the exact shape and structure of the  clouds is hard to discern since we only  see them from one point of view  researchers have now found a new way to  reveal such structures by finding a  molecular cloud that oscillates like  sound in an organ pipe the team observed  muska molecular cloud about 570 light  years from Earth that appears as an  elongated filament using Europe's  Herschel Space Telescope around the  denser filament there appear to be  striations in the guest the astronomers  have suspected might be caused by waves  propagating through the cloud by  analyzing the spacing of the striations  researchers showed that they are caused  by oscillations of the cloud at resonant  frequencies known as normal modes by  modeling such modes and clouds of  various shapes they found that Musca is  not a filament but a sheet viewed  edge-on and could calculate its size the  team hopes that this technique could be  used to study many other clouds and so  shed light on how these stellar  nurseries produce their young  [Music]
 

